  "review": {
    "verdict": "PASS",
    "feedback": "Both statements are correctly scoped. The first theorem assumes 0 < Q, which is needed to make the gcd positive and the quotient expressions the intended delta and K; it proves exactly gcd(delta,K)=1 and does not claim K>0 or delta>=13. The second theorem is a valid Nat identity from M*Q=n*(n-1); it needs no positivity assumption, and its intended application has Q>0. It proves only the scaled identity C*r^2=K*n*(n-1), not the full Pell equation or later bounds. The names are descriptive and do not overstate the proved content."
